(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-quiz"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How are patients managed when they meet modified Duke criteria for possible infective endocarditis?","choices":["Discharged with oral antibiotics","Retested in six months","Treated as having endocarditis","Observed without antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absent extenuating circumstances, patients with definite and possible infective endocarditis are treated as having infective endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Under the modified Duke criteria, Osler nodes count as which minor criterion?","choices":["Immunologic phenomenon","Vascular phenomenon","Microbiologic evidence","Predisposition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes are an immunologic phenomenon, the minor criterion that also holds glomerulonephritis, Roth spots and rheumatoid factor. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which organism causes the most lethal infective endocarditis?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Enterococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal organism, with mortality highest in prosthetic valve endocarditis and lower in people who inject drugs. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which consultant manages antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Palliative care","Infectious disease","Cardiothoracic surgery","Addiction medicine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infectious disease is consulted for antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring pending disposition; cardiothoracic surgery evaluates surgery and anticoagulation needs. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Janeway lesions are particularly associated with which form of infective endocarditis?","choices":["Subacute infective endocarditis","Non-infective (Libman-Sacks) disease","Device infection only, never valves","Acute infective endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Janeway lesions, painless hemorrhagic macules on the palms and soles from septic emboli, are associated with acute infective endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Roughly what share of native valve endocarditis is health care-associated?","choices":["Exactly half","25 to 35%","Under 5%","Over 80%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. About 25-35% of native valve endocarditis is health care-associated, which is why recent hospitalization and lines matter in the history. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which autoimmune disease can produce a non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis that mimics infective endocarditis?","choices":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Reactive arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Systemic lupus erythematosus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic lupus erythematosus may present with non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis, so it sits in the autoimmune differential of infective endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which prophylaxis fits a penicillin-allergic patient who cannot take oral medication?","choices":["Cefazolin or ceftriaxone","Ampicillin injection","Oral cephalexin","Clindamycin injection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin-allergic patients unable to take oral medication receive cefazolin or ceftriaxone intramuscularly or intravenously, provided there is no history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria to penicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Why does a new murmur develop in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Narrowing of a coronary artery by plaque","Valve damage or chordae tendineae rupture","Increased flow from a high-output fever","Pericardial friction from inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A new cardiac murmur is due to valvular damage or rupture of the chordae tendineae, which leads to regurgitation. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"When should empiric antibiotics be started for suspected infective endocarditis?","choices":["Before any cultures, to save time","Only after the echocardiogram","Only once the organism is known","Only after blood cultures are drawn"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy is started only after blood cultures have been drawn, because antibiotics given first can leave the cultures negative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which kind of antibiotic therapy does infective endocarditis require?","choices":["Bactericidal for 3 days","Bacteriostatic and lifelong","Bactericidal and prolonged","Bacteriostatic and brief"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All bacteria in the vegetation must be killed, so therapy must be bactericidal and prolonged, typically 4-6 weeks from the first negative culture, and given parenterally. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"In a child with endocarditis, a fulminant presentation with high spiking fevers suggests which organisms?","choices":["Candida or other fungal species","Pneumococcus or Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci or enterococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Children usually have an indolent course, but a fulminant presentation with high spiking fevers suggests Streptococcus pneumoniae or Staphylococcus aureus. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why does cough feature in infective endocarditis among people who inject drugs?","choices":["Airway irritation from inhaled drugs","Tricuspid septic emboli to the lungs","Mitral regurgitation with pulmonary edema","Aspiration during drug intoxication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs the tricuspid valve is often infected and throws septic emboli to the lungs, causing cough and pleuritic chest pain, with nodular infiltrates on imaging. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which organism causes fungal infective endocarditis?","choices":["Kingella","Eikenella","Enterococcus","Candida"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fungal infective endocarditis is caused by Candida (and Aspergillus); fungal infection is difficult to cure with medication alone and is an indication for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"When is the risk of prosthetic valve endocarditis greatest?","choices":["Within the first year after replacement","Ten or more years after replacement","Only during the operation itself","Equal at every point after surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The greatest risk is within the first year after valve replacement; it then gradually declines until it is similar to the risk for a native valve. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which antibiotic treats infective endocarditis caused by the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group?","choices":["Vancomycin","Rifampin","Ceftriaxone","Nafcillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) endocarditis is treated with ceftriaxone, or ampicillin-sulbactam. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the role of anticoagulation in native valve infective endocarditis?","choices":["It is not beneficial","It prevents all emboli","It is required for all","It dissolves vegetations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation is not beneficial in patients with native valve infection, and it is controversial in patients with prosthetic valves. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which sex is affected more often by infective endocarditis?","choices":["Only children","Men","Women","Equal in both"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Men are affected more often than women, one of the epidemiologic features listed with older age, injection drug use and poor dentition. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In a high-risk patient, what is recommended about prophylaxis for transesophageal echocardiography, upper endoscopy, colonoscopy or cystoscopy?","choices":["It is needed for colonoscopy only","It is given as a 7-day course","It is not recommended without infection","It is required before each one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotic prophylaxis is not recommended for nondental procedures such as transesophageal echocardiography, upper endoscopy, colonoscopy or cystoscopy in the absence of active infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is a perivalvular abscess?","choices":["Infection extending past the leaflets into the myocardium","A sterile clot sitting on a prosthetic valve sewing ring","Pus collecting in the pericardial sac after surgery","A pocket infection around a pacemaker generator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A perivalvular abscess forms when infection extends beyond the valve leaflets into the myocardial tissue around the valve. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which urinalysis findings are expected in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Glucosuria and ketonuria only","Bilirubin and urobilinogen","A completely normal sample","Hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Urinalysis can show hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria, reflecting renal involvement such as glomerulonephritis or emboli. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is bacteremia defined?","choices":["A vegetation seen on transesophageal imaging","A fever above 38 degrees Celsius with chills","Bacteria detected on a true-positive blood culture","Inflammation of the inner lining of the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bacteremia is the presence of bacteria in the bloodstream, identified by detection of any true-positive blood culture. Blood should normally be sterile. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How long do Osler nodes typically last?","choices":["They appear in crops and fade in 2 to 3 days","They persist for months and scar the skin","They last minutes, like an urticarial wheal","They grow steadily until antibiotics stop"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes appear in crops and fade in 2-3 days; they are painful and reflect an immunologic phenomenon. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which prophylaxis fits a patient with no penicillin allergy who cannot take oral medication?","choices":["Oral doxycycline","Intravenous vancomycin","Ampicillin by injection","Clindamycin intravenous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A patient without penicillin allergy who cannot take oral medication gets ampicillin intramuscularly or intravenously 30-60 minutes before the procedure (or cefazolin or ceftriaxone). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which cardiac condition warrants endocarditis prophylaxis before dental work?","choices":["Prior endocarditis","Coronary artery stents","Controlled hypertension","Prior bypass grafting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Previous infective endocarditis is a high-risk condition, with prosthetic valves or valve-repair material, certain congenital heart disease, and cardiac transplant with valve regurgitation. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Infective endocarditis is classified by temporal evolution, by site, by predisposing risk factor and by which other feature?","choices":["The murmur's intensity grade","The cause, such as the organism","The size of the vegetation","The patient's blood type"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Classification uses temporal evolution (acute or subacute), site (native, prosthetic or device), cause of infection (methicillin-resistant or -susceptible Staphylococcus aureus) and predisposing risk factors such as injection drug use. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A gastrointestinal source of bacteremia points to which endocarditis organism?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Kingella species","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Streptococcus gallolyticus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Streptococcus gallolyticus is the gastrointestinal-tract organism among the causes of infective endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is used for methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis of a native valve?","choices":["Ampicillin plus gentamicin","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Vancomycin or daptomycin","Ceftriaxone alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus is treated with nafcillin or oxacillin, or cefazolin; vancomycin is reserved for methicillin-resistant strains. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which organisms cause the largest share of endocarditis on implantable electronic devices?","choices":["Gram-negative coccobacilli","Enterococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Coagulase-negative staphylococci cause about 41% of device endocarditis, with Staphylococcus aureus close behind at about 36%. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Subacute infective endocarditis is gradually progressive unless what happens?","choices":["A major embolus or aneurysm rupture","The patient starts a daily aspirin","The fever rises above 38 degrees","The murmur becomes softer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Subacute endocarditis is gradually progressive unless complicated by a major embolic event or a ruptured mycotic (infected) aneurysm. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
